---
import SourceCode from '../components/SourceCode.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { adapterSource, coreSource, decisions, rapierSource, troikaSource } from '../lib/links';
import { sitePath } from '../lib/site-path';
import { entry, installation, runtime, sharing, teaser, testing, transitions, view } from '../lib/snippets';
---
<Layout title="Guide — effect-three">
  <section class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">A small beginning</p>
    <h1>From Model to mesh.</h1>
    <p>effect-three turns plain scene descriptions into Three.js objects, keeps them in step with your Model, and lets Effect own every lifetime.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="docs-layout">
    <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#installation">Installation</a><a href="#first-scene">Your first scene</a><a href="#foldkit">Using Foldkit</a><a href="#identity">Keys and identity</a><a href="#resources">Resources</a><a href="#animation">Animation</a><a href="#controls">Controls</a><a href="#physics">Physics</a><a href="#text">Text and labels</a><a href="#staging">Staging</a><a href="#xr">XR</a><a href="#runtime">Without Foldkit</a><a href="#testing">Testing</a><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="docs-content">
      <section id="installation">
        <h2>Install the packages.</h2>
        <p>The core works anywhere Effect does. The Foldkit adapter adds the browser pieces: the renderers (WebGL, or WebGPU with <code>webgpuRendererFactory</code>), the animation-frame loop and the scene element.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={installation} lang="sh" sourceId="installation.sh" /></div>
        <p>Working in this monorepo? Use <code>workspace:*</code> for the dependencies. Add <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-rapier</code> for physics and <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-troika</code> for labels when a scene needs them. All the packages are pre-release.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="first-scene">
        <h2>Describe a scene.</h2>
        <p>A scene is plain data: a camera, shared resources and keyed nodes. Constructors fill in defaults, so a description stays short.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={teaser} lang="ts" sourceId="scene.ts" /></div>
        <p>Rotation belongs either to the Model (<code>fixed</code>) or to a behaviour (<code>spin</code>), never both. Behaviours are pure functions of scene time, so frames are deterministic.</p>
        <p>Meshes are built from <code>box</code>, <code>sphere</code>, <code>plane</code>, <code>cylinder</code>, <code>cone</code>, <code>capsule</code>, <code>torus</code>, <code>ring</code>, <code>circle</code> or <code>text</code>; <code>instances</code> draws thousands of copies of one in a single call.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="foldkit">
        <h2>Render it with Foldkit.</h2>
        <p>Create one environment for the application, then render scenes from your view. Pointer interaction comes back as your own typed Messages: clicks, double-clicks, context menus, hover and movement, the wheel, and misses for clearing a selection. Only the handlers you pass are bound.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={entry} lang="ts" sourceId="entry.ts" /></div>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={view} lang="ts" sourceId="view.ts" /></div>
        <p>Unmounting the element releases its renderer, WebGL context, GPU resources, listeners and frame subscription. While Foldkit DevTools time-travels, the scene shows the historical state and ignores pointer input.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/playground/')}>Try the playground <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="identity">
        <h2>Keys keep objects alive.</h2>
        <p>Keys are unique across the whole scene. A node keeps its Three.js object while its key and kind stay the same, including when it moves to another group. A structurally equal scene costs no host changes at all, which matters because Foldkit sends a fresh scene on every render.</p>
        <p>Updating 1,000 meshes takes under 100 µs, whether nothing changed or one property did; then exactly one operation is applied.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="resources">
        <h2>Share what the GPU holds.</h2>
        <p>Declare a geometry or material once in <code>resources</code>, then refer to it by id. Shared resources are reference-counted across every scene on the page and disposed when their last user goes. Owned resources live and die with their mesh.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={sharing} lang="ts" sourceId="shared.ts" /></div>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/shared/')}>Try shared resources <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="animation">
        <h2>Animate without update.</h2>
        <p>Behaviours run in the frame loop, outside Foldkit's update, so a spinning scene never causes a Model update. Scenes pause their frames when off screen.</p>
        <p>Transitions ease <code>position</code> and <code>scale</code> to whatever the Model says next. A new target mid-flight starts from the pose on screen, and a node moving to another group keeps its place in the world while it travels.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={transitions} lang="ts" sourceId="transitions.ts" /></div>
        <p>Under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, behaviours freeze and transitions land at once while frames keep rendering.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/playground/')}>Watch cubes glide between shelves <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="controls">
        <h2>Keep the camera in the Model.</h2>
        <p>With <code>{"scene({ gestures: 'scene' })"}</code> the scene claims drags, the wheel and pinches, and reports each as a <code>Gesture</code> Message. <code>orbit(model.camera, gesture)</code> returns the next camera, as three.js's <code>OrbitControls</code> would move it, zooming toward the pointer if you ask. <code>orbitKey</code> handles arrow keys, and <code>fitCamera</code> frames a box the scene measured for you, as Drei's <code>Bounds</code> does.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/studio/')}>Orbit the studio <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="physics">
        <h2>Describe bodies; let Rapier move them.</h2>
        <p>Give a mesh, group, loaded model or instances node a <code>body()</code> under <code>{"scene({ physics: physics() })"}</code>, and pass <code>loadRapier()</code> from <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-rapier</code> to the environment. Colliders come from geometry. Kicks are impulses with an id, applied once. Joints (fixed, spherical, revolute, prismatic, rope, spring, with motors) are data in <code>{"physics({ joints })"}</code>. Give a body colliders of its own when its geometry is the wrong shape. Collisions and contact forces return as Messages naming the node, and the instance when there is one, and <code>{"physics({ debug: true })"}</code> draws every collider.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/toybox/')}>Throw the toys around <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="text">
        <h2>Set text two ways.</h2>
        <p><code>text()</code> is geometry from a typeface font, as Drei's <code>Text3D</code>: extruded or flat, with letter spacing, line height and alignment. <code>label()</code> is crisp distance-field text at any size, as Drei's <code>Text</code>, drawn by troika once you pass <code>troikaLabels()</code> from <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-troika</code>. Labels wrap and take anchors and outlines; give them a TTF, OTF or WOFF font.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/workshop/')}>See both in the workshop <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="staging">
        <h2>Light and stage it.</h2>
        <p><code>environment()</code>, <code>sky()</code> and <code>contactShadows()</code> are Drei's staging as scene fields, and <code>shaderGrid()</code> is its fading grid. <code>stage()</code> returns Drei's <code>Stage</code> lighting to spread into a scene. Centre content with <code>centerOffset</code> on a measured box, and load one of Drei's environment presets with <code>environmentPresetUrl</code>.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/playground/')}>Stand on the fading grid <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="xr">
        <h2>Step into it.</h2>
        <p>A scene with <code>{"xr({ origin, heading })"}</code> can be presented in WebXR. Enter and exit with <code>environment.xr</code> Effects from your Commands. Controllers and hands hover, click and drag along their rays. Thumbsticks and buttons arrive as Messages: <code>locomote</code> turns and steps the player by returning new <code>xr</code> settings for your Model. In AR, <code>{"xr({ hitTest: true })"}</code> reports the real surfaces a select points at.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/toybox/')}>Enter VR in the toybox <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="runtime">
        <h2>Use the runtime directly.</h2>
        <p>Outside Foldkit, start a scene inside a Scope. Startup is atomic: if the description is invalid, the renderer can't be created, or startup is interrupted, nothing is left behind.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={runtime} lang="ts" sourceId="runtime.ts" /></div>
      </section>
      <section id="testing">
        <h2>Test it headless.</h2>
        <p>A recording renderer, a manual frame scheduler and Effect's TestClock make scene tests deterministic, with no browser and no GPU.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={testing} lang="ts" sourceId="scene.test.ts" /></div>
      </section>
      <section id="architecture">
        <h2>How it fits together.</h2>
        <div class="module-grid">
          {[
            [coreSource + '/src/kernel', 'Kernel', 'Plain TypeScript: descriptions, keyed diff, applier, resources and behaviours. It never imports Effect.', 'effect-three/kernel'],
            [coreSource + '/src/runtime', 'Runtime', 'Effect services, scopes and typed errors around the kernel.', 'effect-three'],
            [adapterSource, 'Foldkit adapter', 'The scene element, WebGL renderer, frame loop, XR sessions and DOM bridge.', 'effect-three-foldkit'],
            [rapierSource, 'Rapier physics', 'The physics engine: bodies, colliders and joints described in scenes, simulated by Rapier.', 'effect-three-rapier'],
            [troikaSource, 'Troika labels', 'The label engine: distance-field text drawn by troika.', 'effect-three-troika'],
            [decisions, 'Decisions', 'The plan, architecture decision records and benchmark results.', 'docs/effect-three'],
          ].map(([href, name, description, path]) => <a href={href}>
            <h3>{name} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></h3><p>{description}</p><code>{path}</code>
          </a>)}
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
